AU  - Liu, D; Yu, CL; Jiang, LX; Feng, R
TI  - Multiscale synergy of solar-induced chlorophyll fluorescence and normalized difference vegetation index enhances maize chilling injury monitoring: a remote sensing verification in Northeast China
T2  - JOURNAL OF APPLIED REMOTE SENSING
LA  - English
KW  - solar-induced chlorophyll fluorescence; MODIS NDVI; chilling injury; Gram-Schmidt fusion; precision agriculture; Northeast China; SURFACE AIR-TEMPERATURE; MODIS DATA; CLIMATE; DYNAMICS; PHOTOSYNTHESIS
AB  - Northeast China, a critically important region for global maize production, faces increasing risks of chilling damage due to the northward expansion of cultivation under climate warming. To address the limitations of conventional monitoring methods, we developed an integrated "physiology-remote sensing-disaster" framework. We generated a daily, 250-m resolution solar-induced chlorophyll fluorescence (SIF) product by fusing Sentinel-5P TROPOSIF (743-758 nm) with Moderate Resolution Imaging Spectroradiometer (MODIS) normalized difference vegetation index (NDVI) data (quantification consistency index, lambda=0.8849). This downscaled SIF product was then combined with soil moisture data and applied within a deep learning model to identify maize chilling injury. Our results demonstrated that the "TROPOSIF-soil moisture" combination achieved superior accuracy in identifying chilling damage compared to the traditional "NDVI-soil moisture" approach, particularly during the tasseling-milking stage, where it matched 89% of ground observations in core damage areas. Furthermore, the correlation between NDVI and SIF strengthened with longer temporal scales (growing-season R-2=0.597 versus 10-day R-2=0.529), underscoring the role of canopy structure in long-term assessments. The downscaling performance was higher in homogeneous plains (lambda>0.83) than in heterogeneous mountainous-grassland zones (lambda=0.31 to 0.90), revealing the impact of land surface heterogeneity on data fusion. Although the method showed high accuracy during reproductive stages, its performance was limited at the seedling stage due to low canopy coverage. We provide a scalable and physiologically sensitive tool for improving chilling injury monitoring, offering significant potential for applications in climate-resilient precision agriculture.
