AU  - Dai, TJ; Chen, JY; Liu, H; Liu, L; Fan, SL; Bai, XQ; Qian, L; Ba, YL; Qiongda
TI  - Research on the potential of reconstructing spectral indices and dividing crop growth stages based on satellite remote sensing for monitoring soil moisture in farmland
T2  - COMPUTERS AND ELECTRONICS IN AGRICULTURE
LA  - English
KW  - Sentinel-2; Feature Space; Soil Moisture Content; Crop Growth Stages; Machine Learning; VEGETATION INDEX; INVERSION; ALGORITHM; CLASSIFICATION; SENTINEL-2; INFORMATION; EXTRACTION; FEATURES; OPTRAM; MODEL
AB  - Soil moisture content (SMC) is a critical factor influencing agricultural production and ecosystem health. Accurately and rapidly obtaining SMC in farmland plays a critical role in improving irrigation efficiency and optimizing water resource allocation. Previous research mostly focused on monitoring SMC during a specific growth stage of crops or the entire growing season, neglecting the differences that exist during the crop growth process. This study aims to investigate the impact of different crop growth stages on the estimation of SMC in farmland. Taking the 8th Agricultural Division of the Xinjiang Production and Construction Corps as the study area, soil samples at varying depths were collected from May to July 2023, and vegetation indices and texture features were extracted based on concurrent Sentinel-2 satellite imagery. Based on the growth status of crops in the study area, the following measures were taken: setting control period D0 (all data without distinguishing crop growth stages) and experimental periods D1 (early growth stage of crops), D2 (mid-growth stage of crops), D3 (late growth stage of crops). To improve the correlation between variables and SMC, spectral reflectance is reconstructed using a two-dimensional spectral feature space, and the best subsets of reconstructed spectral reflectance (RV), reconstructed vegetation indexs (RVIS), and texture features (TFS) are selected for different periods and depths. Soil moisture inversion models were constructed using Support Vector Machine (SVM), Genetic Algorithm-Optimized Back Propagation Neural Network (GA-BP), and Extreme Learning Machine (ELM). The results show that: (1) The reconstructed spectral reflectance mainly improves the correlation between variables and SMC as well as the model accuracy in the middle and late stages of crop growth; (2) The optimal inversion depths for D0, D1, and D2 periods are all 0-10 cm, while for the D3 period, the optimal inversion depth is 10-20 cm; (3) ELM is the optimal inversion model for the D1 period, while SVM is the optimal inversion model for the D2 and D3 periods; (4) The division of crop growth stages improves the inversion accuracy of the model, with R2 increasing by a maximum of 0.36, reaching up to 0.67. The results of this study can provide scientific support for real-time dynamic monitoring of SMC and precision agriculture.
