AU  - Zhang, ZX; Qian, L; Qiu, YC; Shi, ZW; Sun, YH; Xu, W
TI  - An Automated Method for Regional-Scale Agricultural Soil Moisture Retrieval Using ISMN Measurements and Sentinel Data in Google Earth Engine
T2  - IEEE JOURNAL OF SELECTED TOPICS IN APPLIED EARTH OBSERVATIONS AND REMOTE SENSING
LA  - English
KW  - Soil moisture; Moisture; Soil; Remote sensing; Sentinel-1; Monitoring; Data models; Soil measurements; Microwave radiometry; Vegetation mapping; Fusion of optical and SAR data; Google Earth Engine (GEE); machine learning; soil moisture estimation; RANDOM FOREST; SMAP; NETWORK; PRODUCTS; IMAGES; RADAR
AB  - As a critical geophysical parameter governing land-atmosphere interactions, soil moisture (SM) plays a pivotal role in regulating global water and energy cycles. Despite the proliferation of satellite-derived SM products, their utility in precision agriculture remains constrained by inherent limitations in spatial resolution and cross-regional generalizability. This study presents a novel machine learning framework that synergistically integrates multisource earth observation data with in-situ measurements from the International Soil Moisture Network (ISMN) to address these limitations. Leveraging the cloud-based computational capabilities of Google Earth Engine (GEE), we developed a high-resolution (60m) SM estimation model through systematically synergistic integration of Sentinel-2 multispectral reflectance, Sentinel-1 C-band SAR backscatter characteristics (VV, VH polarization, incidence angle) and ISMN ground measurements. The microwave-canopy interaction mechanisms were quantitatively characterized using the Michigan Microwave Canopy Scattering (MIMICS) model, informing the selection of optimal predictor variables from Sentinel-2 MSI spectral bands (B1-B8A, B9-B12) and SoilGrids texture parameters. A streamlined machine learning regression architecture was implemented, achieving optimized performance through feature importance analysis that identified clay content (32.98%) and sand content (28.25%) as dominant predictors. Validation across heterogeneous agricultural regions demonstrated robust performance (Bias < 0.05 cm(3)/cm(3)) in low-to-moderate moisture regimes (< 0.36 cm(3)/cm(3)), with systematic underestimation observed in saturated conditions. The operational pipeline generated monthly composite SM maps for Henan Province croplands (April 2018), establishing a transferable paradigm for global-scale high-resolution SM monitoring, particularly in low-stature crop environments (e.g., winter wheat). This methodology advances the synergistic use of microwave-optical-soil property data fusion and provides critical insights for extending the framework to other vegetation types.
