AU  - Tong, X.; Zhu, J.
TI  - Spatial coupling of land use and atmospheric methane (XCH4) in the Tumen River Basin
T2  - Proceedings of SPIE - The International Society for Optical Engineering
LA  - English
KW  - FROM-GLC; land use; methane; spatial heterogeneity; TROPOMI; Tumen River Basin; valley effect; XCH<sub>4</sub>; Belts; Catchments; Crops; Forestry; Remote sensing; Rivers; Soil moisture; Watersheds; Wetlands; Atmospheric methanes; Dominant class; FROM-GLC; River basins; Spatial couplings; Spatial heterogeneity; TROPOMI; Tumen river basin; Valley effect; XCH4; Land use; Methane
AB  - This study focuses on 2024 and combines 30 m FROM-GLC land-cover data (clipped to the Tumen River Basin) with Sentinel-5P/TROPOMI XCH4, retaining native footprint resolution and summarizing by both dominant-class (>50%) and area-weighted approaches to characterize column-averaged methane concentration (XCH4,ppb) over different land-use types. The basin is dominated by forest (85.04%) and cropland (12.34%), with built-up land at 2.07%. Annual-mean XCH4 spans 1789.05-1944.40 ppb, forming belt-like and patchy highs along mid-/down-valley corridors and hotspots near wetlands/paddy clusters. Class-mean XCH4(dominant-class) is relatively high over built-up (1902.86), cropland (1900.79), and water (1901.62), lowest over forest (1884.37), and intermediate over grassland (1895.94); shrubland (1904.72) and wetland (1901.10) also show high means but occupy ≤0.01% of the basin, thus having limited impact on the basin average. Topography-surface-boundary-layer coupling amplifies the valley-mountain contrast. Methodologically, avoiding up-sampling prevents pseudo-fine artifacts; all metrics are concentrations (ppb) rather than fluxes. The findings support actionable monitoring and management: prioritize observations in valley cropland- settlement belts and wet/impounded environments, promote intermittent irrigation and mid-season drainage, and conduct annual evaluations using 2024 as a baseline with soil-moisture and crop-distribution covariates.  © 2026 SPIE.
