AU  - Sampelli, A.; Khandare, A.; Muvva Venkata, R.; Karmakar, S.; Ghosh, S.
TI  - Atmospheric aridity perturbs critical soil moisture thresholds of plant water stress over Indian biomes
T2  - Advances in Water Resources
LA  - English
KW  - Critical soil moisture threshold; Evaporative fraction; Soil moisture; Vapor pressure deficit; India; Atmospheric thermodynamics; Forestry; Irrigation; Latent heat; Rain; Soil surveys; Transpiration; Vapor pressure; %moisture; Critical soil moisture threshold; Energy; Evaporative fraction; Gross primary productivity; Land-atmosphere interactions; Moisture level; Plant water stress; Vapor pressure deficit; agricultural application; biome; evaporation; hydrological regime; irrigation; land-atmosphere interaction; latent heat flux; plant water relations; primary production; quantitative analysis; rainfed agriculture; satellite data; seasonal variation; sensible heat flux; soil moisture; threshold; transpiration; vapor pressure; water availability; water stress; Soil moisture
AB  - Understanding the critical soil moisture threshold (θcrit)- the soil moisture (SM) level at which the soil-plant continuum shifts from energy-limited to water-limited during drydowns is essential for comprehending land-atmosphere interactions in determining transpiration limits and vegetation productivity. Using Soil Moisture Active Passive satellite data, we quantified θcrit for Indian biomes employing two independent methodologies. The first method demonstrates the dependence of evaporative fraction (EF), the ratio of latent heat to sum of latent and sensible heat fluxes, on SM (EF-SM). The second method relies on analyzing the covariance between gross primary productivity (GPP) and vapor pressure deficit (VPD) under varying SM conditions (Cov(GPP-VPD)-SM). We find that, in comparison to the EF-SM method, the Cov(GPP-VPD)-SM approach is more sensitive to changes in θcrit, for assessing seasonal dynamics and the impacts of varying hydrological forcings. Seasonal variations in θcrit across India using the Cov(GPP-VPD)-SM method showed that θcrit is 0.1205 ± 0.0016 m³/m³ higher during monsoon than the non-monsoon season. Additionally, θcrit during drydowns following rainfall is higher by 0.0377 ± 0.0012 m³/m³ than during irrigation-driven drydowns. Higher (lower) VPD observed in non-monsoon (monsoon) or irrigation-driven (rain-driven) scenarios play a significant role in shaping θcrit. The seasonal and rain-irrigation variations of θcrit driven by VPD underscore the critical importance of incorporating VPD into methodologies for computing θcrit. Moreover, the observed distinctions between rainfed and irrigated conditions underscore the importance of employing methods that account for unique hydrological and management characteristics present in diverse agricultural contexts. © 2025
