TI  - Remote sensing of soil moisture in agroecosystems
T2  - Agricultural Applications of Earth Observation
LA  - English
KW  - Correlation analyses; evapotranspiration; soil water; surface temperature; water balance; Carbon; Correlation methods; Data assimilation; Decision making; Ecosystems; Infrared radiation; Learning algorithms; Learning systems; Microwave sensors; Precision agriculture; Remote sensing; Soil moisture; Soil surveys; Space optics; Surface temperature; Water resources; %moisture; Active/passive; Agro-ecosystems; Correlation analysis; Remote-sensing; Sentinel-1; Soil moisture monitoring; Soil water; Surface temperatures; Water balance; Economic and social effects
AB  - Soil moisture (SM) plays a critical role in regulating the terrestrial water cycle, carbon and nitrogen fluxes, and energy balance. Advancements in remote sensing have significantly improved SM monitoring, transitioning from traditional in situ measurements to multiscale satellite observations. This chapter explores the physical principles, retrieval methodologies, and applications of remote sensing-based SM estimation, with a focus on its role in cropland water resource management. We first examined the trade-offs in microwave sensing, including the inherent resolution-penetration compromise between passive (e.g., Soil Moisture Active Passive [SMAP]/Soil Moisture and Ocean Salinity [SMOS]) and active (e.g., Sentinel-1 SAR) sensor systems. We then examined optical-thermal infrared methods that exploit vegetation-temperature feature spaces, along with microwave data to enhance SM retrieval. Recent advancements in multisensor fusion (e.g., SMAP-Sentinel-1 downscaling) are presented as promising solutions to reconcile spatiotemporal scale gaps, enabling field-scale SM mapping with sub-kilometer resolution. Additionally, machine learning techniques are demonstrated to enhance retrieval accuracy by integrating diverse data sources. This chapter also highlights advanced applications of remotely sensed SM in agricultural decision-making. We discuss the role of remote sensing-based SM in optimizing irrigation strategies through data assimilation techniques and inverse modeling approaches. Furthermore, we explore the integration of remote sensing-derived SM into carbon cycle models, facilitating improved estimates of ecosystem respiration and gross primary productivity under varying SM regimes. By synthesizing recent advancements in remote sensing technology and computational methods, this chapter provides a comprehensive overview of how SM monitoring can enhance sustainable cropland water management, improve climate resilience, and support precision agriculture. The insights presented in this study contribute to the development of robust remote sensing frameworks for addressing water scarcity and optimizing agricultural productivity. © 2026 Elsevier Ltd. All rights reserved.
