AU  - Vetrivel, S.C.; Sabareeshwari, V.; Sowmiya, K.C.; Arun, V.P.
TI  - Data-Driven Approaches for Sustainable Agriculture and Food Security
T2  - Optimizing AI Applications for Sustainable Agriculture
LA  - English
KW  - big data analytics; Data-driven approaches; food security; Internet of Things (IoT); precision agriculture; sustainable agriculture; Advanced Analytics; Agricultural machinery; Big data; Crops; Food security; Food supply; Learning algorithms; Precision agriculture; Resource allocation; Soil moisture; Soil surveys; Sustainable agriculture; Big data analytic; Data analytics; Data driven; Data-driven approach; Food security; Internet of thing; Precision Agriculture; Resources allocation; Sustainable agriculture; Weather patterns; Remote sensing
AB  - The advent of data-driven methods is successfully addressing the issues of food security and sustainable agriculture. By 2050, there will likely be 9.7 billion people on the planet, making it critical to increase agricultural output while reducing negative environmental effects. This summary offers insights into the transformative impact of data-driven approaches on the agricultural sector, contributing significantly to the attainment of sustainable agriculture and food security objectives. A crucial element of data-driven methodologies involves gathering and analyzing extensive agricultural data. High-resolution data covering a range of agricultural topics, such as soil conditions, weather patterns, crop growth, and insect infestations, has been made easier by developments in sensor technology, satellite photography, and remote sensing. These data sources enable farmers and policy-makers to make informed choices about crop selection, pest management tactics, and resource allocation when combined with machine learning algorithms and data analytics. Precision agriculture, which applies site-specific interventions to maximize crop yields while limiting resource inputs like water, fertilizer, and pesticides, is also made possible by data-driven methodologies. Farmers can adjust their methods to meet the particular needs of each field by analyzing data on crop health, nutrient levels, and soil moisture. By reducing water and chemical waste, this focused strategy not only increases output but also lessens the environmental effect of agriculture. In addition to optimizing resource allocation, data-driven approaches contribute to enhancing resilience in agriculture. Given the significant challenges posed by climate change to food security, including the heightened frequency and severity of extreme weather events, analyzing historical climate data and utilizing predictive models allow farmers to anticipate weather patterns. This proactive approach enables them to adjust planting and harvesting schedules accordingly, minimizing crop losses and ensuring a stable food supply. © 2025 Scrivener Publishing LLC.
