AU  - Sarkar, A.; Sanyal, A.; Basak, S.
TI  - Community-based sustainable water management practices, strategy, technology, and innovation: A significant mouza scale study in foothill of Garpanchakot, West Bengal
T2  - Developments in Environmental Science
LA  - English
KW  - Agricultural soil science; Environmental geography; Human habitat; Hydrology; Natural resources
AB  - Community-based water management practices are not just a strategy but a testament to the invaluable role of local communities in decision-making, planning, and implementation of water resource management. This approach recognizes and celebrates the importance of local people who possess unique information and knowledge about the climate and landscape of their region. This study is designed to evaluate the condition of climate and landscape and people's traditional water management practices in this area. It also aims to assess how to initiate and enhance community-based sustainable water management practices using modern tools and technology. The study aims to reduce the water crisis during the dry season and prepare a strategy for mitigation and resilience in the tribal community. Twenty-two Census villages surrounded by Garpanchakot Hill have been selected for the study. SRTM DEM has been utilized to analyze the landscape. Digital cartographic techniques have been employed for settlement and waterbody mapping. Multispectral satellite data were analyzed to prepare Normalized Difference Vegetation Index and Normalize Difference Water Index. Change detection is also done through two different years of multispectral data. Highly populated villages in the Garpanchakot foothills suffer severe water crises during summer. However, we believe that community-based water management practices can be a beacon of hope in these challenging times. The only water source in this area is monsoonal rainfall, which occurs 2–3 months a year. Surface water accumulated during this short span of rainfall is utilized throughout the year for agriculture and other purposes. As it is part of the erosion plateau Chhotanagpur, groundwater is rare. The soil of the foothills is sandy loam in character. Those areas are suitable for rainwater harvesting. Several natural water bodies have been spotted in this area for storing rainwater. Several artificial water bodies have been created to store water during the rainy season. Local communities undeniably hold invaluable knowledge about their environments, encompassing traditional conservation practices, indigenous irrigation methods, and a profound understanding of local hydrology. Integrating this knowledge into decision-making is imperative to pave the way for effective and culturally sensitive solutions. Community-based water management practices bolster communities' capacity through rigorous training, education, and resource allocation and ensure long-term sustainability. These endeavors prioritize efficient resource use through unwavering conservation measures such as rainwater harvesting systems, drip irrigation techniques, and soil moisture retention. Additionally, they fervently focus on ecosystems that provide essential services such as watershed protection and wetland preservation, reassuring the audience about the effectiveness of the proposed solutions. © 2025 Elsevier Inc.
