KW  - artificial intelligence; deep learning; Machine learning; remote sensing; smart agriculture; Agricultural machinery; Convolutional neural networks; Crops; Decision making; Image recognition; Learning algorithms; Learning systems; Smart agriculture; Soil moisture; Soil surveys; Agricultural management; Agricultural monitoring; Agricultural yields; Case-studies; Crop classification; Deep learning; Machine-learning; Plantings; Remote-sensing; Smart agricultures; Deep learning; Remote sensing
AB  - Deep learning (DL) algorithms have the potential to revolutionize agricultural management and monitoring, as this paper explains. Optimized planting and harvesting tactics are now possible thanks to DL’s impressive improvement in predicting agricultural yields using previous weather, soil, and crop data. Convolutional neural networks (CNNs) and other image recognition algorithms can examine high-resolution photos of crops for indications of pest infestations or diseases, enabling timely and accurate interventions. By allowing focused treatments within specific field areas, these new approaches improve resource efficiency in the context of precision agriculture, drastically lowering waste. To help with real-time monitoring of crop development, soil moisture, and other important environmental conditions, the article also explains how DL may be used to analyze large volumes of data from remote sensing and satellite photography. To help develop successful adaptation measures, sophisticated algorithms reveal the possible effects of climate change on agriculture. Also explored are robotics enabled by DL for automated sorting and harvesting, which hold the potential for more efficiency and less labor expenses. In addition, there is hope that machine learning models can optimize the whole agricultural supply chain, leading to less waste and better products. Lastly, the study emphasizes the impact of DL in collecting data from many sources, such as weather stations and satellites, to create all-encompassing monitoring systems that enable decision-making in real-time. © 2026 Elsevier Inc. All rights reserved..
