TI  - Estimating soil moisture at farm scale with high spatial resolution: integrating remote sensing data, and machine learning
T2  - Journal of Hydrology
LA  - English
KW  - Data fusion; Machine learning (ML); Remote sensing; Soil moisture; Agricultural machinery; Agriculture; Boundary conditions; Data mining; Evapotranspiration; Groundwater; Groundwater resources; Image resolution; Learning systems; Soil surveys; Textures; %moisture; Actual evapotranspiration; Ground water depths; High spatial resolution; Machine learning; Machine-learning; Moisture data; Remote-sensing; Surface soil moisture; Upper boundary; image resolution; machine learning; remote sensing; soil moisture; spatial resolution; Remote sensing; Soil moisture
AB  - High-quality, long-term surface soil moisture (SSM) data are crucial for agricultural water management. However, existing remote sensing soil moisture products typically have coarse spatial and temporal resolutions, generally at the kilometer scale, which limits their ability to capture the complex spatiotemporal dynamics of agricultural areas. Given that actual evapotranspiration (ET) and groundwater depth (GWD) are essential upper and lower boundary conditions for determining SSM, we propose an SSM downscaling framework that transfers knowledge learned at a 9 km resolution to generate estimates at a 30 m resolution. This approach first employs a Gradient Boosting Decision Tree (GBDT) model using inputs such as actual ET, GWD, soil texture, land surface temperature (LST), normalized difference vegetation index (NDVI), and albedo to estimate SSM at the coarse scale. The trained model is then applied to input factors at 30 m resolution to generate high-resolution SSM data. The framework was evaluated using continuous monitoring data from three sites and soil water content observations from 59 regional sampling points. Validation results demonstrate strong performance, with a mean R of 0.658, MAE of 0.014, and RMSE of 0.021 cm3/cm3on the temporal scale; and a mean R of 0.672, MAE of 0.029, and RMSE of 0.061 cm3/cm3on the spatial scale. Furthermore, incorporating ET and GWD improved accuracy by up to 70.3 %, particularly in irrigated areas where shallow groundwater drives soil moisture and irrigation inputs are difficult to quantify. By integrating both upper and lower boundary conditions, the proposed framework effectively captures field-scale SSM dynamics and enables high-resolution SSM monitoring in complex agricultural environments. In addition, the framework demonstrates strong extrapolation capability across different datasets and can be applied to higher spatial resolution data products, such as CLDAS. © 2025 Elsevier B.V. All rights are reserved, including those for text and data mining, AI training, and similar technologies.
