TI  - Enhancing agricultural water management through sensor-based irrigation systems
T2  - Toxicological and Environmental Chemistry
LA  - English
KW  - Agriculture; climate variability; crop water requirements; sensor-based irrigation; soil moisture sensors; sustainability; water management; weather stations; Crops; Cultivation; Food supply; Irrigation; Remote sensing; Sustainable agriculture; Water conservation; Agricultural activities; Agricultural water management; Climate variability; Crop production; Crop water requirements; Irrigation systems; Sensor-based irrigation; Soil moisture sensors; Waters managements; Weather stations; agricultural application; climate variation; irrigation system; remote sensing; soil moisture; sustainability; water management; water use; Sustainable development; Water management
AB  - Water efficiently is used in agricultural activities which is an important part of sustainable crop production, especially in regions which are facing water shortages and wide climate variability. Sensor based irrigation systems are an advancement technology which provide us timely information about the moisture content in the soil, the prevailing weather conditions and the crops water needs. In this paper, a sensor-enabled irrigation system implementation is being considered for water usage efficiency in agricultural setup. The chapter discusses water management in agriculture, emphasizing the need for novel technologies like remote sensors. It details sensor-driven irrigation systems, their deployment, and their importance in water conservation. The paper discusses the improvement in crop yields, reduced water wastage and environmental sustainability due to sensor-based irrigation. It reinforces the principle of education or training for farmers to intelligently manage them so that the associated advantages can be fully reached. Eventually, this paper is keen on offering comprehensive background information about sensor-based irrigation systems and their undeniable ability to upgrade agricultural water management, preserve water resources, and be incorporated in food security issues for the sake of changing in climate supporting. © 2025 Informa UK Limited, trading as Taylor & Francis Group.
