AU  - Ekadu, S.; Barker, B.; Yost, M.; Flint, E.
TI  - Leveraging Technology for Enhancing Irrigation Management in the Colorado River Basin of Utah for a Water-Secure Future
T2  - 2025 ASABE Annual International Meeting
LA  - English
KW  - Agricultural Technology; Decision Support Systems; Drought Resilience; Precision Irrigation Remote Sensing; Water Conservation; Agribusiness; Agricultural technology; Decision making; Drought; Population statistics; Soil conservation; Soil moisture; Sprinkler systems (irrigation); Subirrigation; Water management; Watersheds; Agricultural technologies; Colorado River Basin; Decision supports; Drought resilience; Fundings; Irrigation management; Precision irrigation; Precision irrigation remote sensing; Remote-sensing; Support systems; Decision support systems; Water conservation
AB  - The Colorado River Basin (CRB), a critical water source for 40 million people and vast agricultural lands across the Western U.S., faces an unprecedented crisis driven by historical overallocation, prolonged drought, and climate change-induced aridification. Utah, an Upper Basin state heavily reliant on CRB water, particularly for its dominant agricultural sector (70-80% of state water use), confronts mounting pressure to optimize water management amidst scarcity and population growth. This paper examines the potential of leveraging advanced technologies-including Internet of Things (IoT) sensors (soil moisture, weather, flow meters), remote sensing platforms like OpenET for evapotranspiration (ET) estimation, precision application methods (variable rate irrigation, low-elevation center pivot sprinklers, drip), automation, and decision support systems-to enhance irrigation management within Utah's CRB. It highlights state initiatives like the Utah Department of Agriculture and Food's Agricultural Water Optimization Funding Program and presents the Ag-Water Demonstration, Research, and Implementation Program (AG-DRIP) as a focused case study demonstrating an integrated approach to technology deployment, farmer engagement, and support. Achieving a water-secure future necessitates a holistic strategy that integrates technological advancements with supportive water policies (addressing water rights and incentivizing verified conservation), sustained funding, robust education and extension efforts, and continued research focused on quantifying impacts and refining context-specific solutions. © 2025 ASABE. All rights reserved.
