KW  - Deep Learning; LSTM; Precision Agriculture; Remote Sensing; Sentinel-2; Soil Moisture Prediction; Vegetation Indices; Agricultural machinery; Climate change; Complex networks; Extreme weather; Irrigation; Learning systems; Long short-term memory; Precision agriculture; Satellite imagery; Soil moisture; Soil surveys; Space optics; Vegetation; Weather forecasting; Weather satellites; Deep learning; LSTM; Model method; Modeling and forecasting; Precision Agriculture; Precision irrigation; Remote-sensing; Sentinel-2; Soil moisture predictions; Vegetation index; Remote sensing
AB  - This research confronts the escalating challenges in precision irrigation driven by climate change by proposing an advanced machine learning framework. The system integrates high-resolution multispectral imagery from Sentinel-2 satellites, specifically leveraging vegetation indices such as NDVI, NDMI, and LAI, with comprehensive meteorological data from OpenMeteo to forecast soil moisture content. A multibranch deep learning architecture was developed, which evaluates LSTM recurrent neural networks, to effectively model the complex temporal dynamics inherent in agricultural environments. Analysis of a substantial dataset revealed that the LSTM-based model demonstrated superior capability in capturing long-term dependencies and showed a moderate predictive strength. However, the model also highlighted difficulties in accurately predicting extreme values. Feature importance analysis indicated that satellite-derived vegetation indices (e.g., fcover count, ndvi count) exerted a more significant influence on predictions than meteorological inputs. This study contributes to enhancing the precision of irrigation scheduling and optimizing resource use for sustainable agriculture. Future research directions include the integration of detailed soil characteristics, specific indicators for extreme weather events, and the exploration of hybrid model architectures to improve predictive accuracy, particularly for outlier conditions, and to ensure robustness across varied agro-ecological zones.  © 2025 IEEE.
