TY  - BOOK_CHAPTER
AU  - Tanna, A.B.; Jatakia, R.S.; Nagare, G.D.
TI  - Role of AI in the Field of Precision Agriculture
T2  - Artificial Intelligence for Precision Agriculture
LA  - English
AB  - One of the oldest and most important sectors is agriculture. For the vast majority of people on earth, it serves as their primary source of sustenance. Farmers seek new ways to meet the demand due to the increasing population growth and labour shortage. This could be resolved by Precision Agriculture (PA). It is also referred to as Precision Farming or Site-Specific Crop Management (SSCM), which is a farming management concept based on the idea of collecting real-time data using Information Technology (IT) and remote sensing software to ensure optimum soil and crop health. The parameters that need to be focused on for good crop growth and productive yield are optimal planting times, harvesting times, soil fertility, soil moisture, weather conditions, labour costs and equipment management and availability. PA enables efficient procurement of the mentioned data and uses mobile apps to convey its predictions and guidance to the farmers. Nowadays, rapid environmental changes and changing landscape results in huge amounts of resource wastage and damaged agricultural outputs which has an adverse effect on the farmers' livelihood. Introduction to PA in modern agriculture has brought effectual improvement in such circumstances, making room for better yields, healthy agricultural fields and reduced additional expenses for farmers. Artificial Intelligence (AI) is a promising technology introduced by the computational and IT industry which can be used extensively in the agricultural industry. It can bring subsequent development in remote sensing technologies and predictive analysis software, the two main components of Precision Agriculture Technology. AI has been implemented in agriculture to increase opportunities for the development in intelligent agricultural systems. Machine learning (ML) is another application of AI that can bring immense development and substantial changes in modern agriculture with its ability to learn from large amounts of data and adjust to new inputs without any programming. Application of AI in PA can give constructive and effective help to farmers in subjects of soil management, types of seeds to be planted according to the weather conditions, fertilizers that can be productive in different soil types, and in weeds and pest management. It helps in collecting large volumes of data for ensuring better soil and crop health. AI assists farmers in various other factors such as object detection, disease detection, pest detection, labour management and equipment management. Soil monitoring, crop management, weather monitoring and field scouting are major tasks that take a good amount of time and resources, and AI helps farmers to accomplish these tasks with productive and efficient results. It does involve a considerable amount of challenges as well such as heavy investment for software, lack of time and technical skills for farmers to get effective outcomes from AI solutions and less awareness of such technologies in rural areas. Despite such limitations, AI and PA remain to be one of the most successful applications in the agricultural industry and its increasing popularity can bring great developments in the agricultural sector. © 2025 Taylor & Francis Group, LLC.
