TI  - Study of land cover/land use changes using RS and GIS: a case study of Multan district, Pakistan
T2  - Environmental Monitoring and Assessment
LA  - English
KW  - Climate change; Crops; Land surface temperature (LST); NDVI; Remote sensing; Environmental Monitoring; Geographic Information Systems; Pakistan; Plants; Remote Sensing Technology; Seasons; Temperature; Urbanization; Multan; Pakistan; Punjab [Pakistan]; Gossypium hirsutum; Triticum aestivum; Atmospheric temperature; Climate change; Crops; Food supply; Geographic information systems; Remote sensing; Surface measurement; Surface properties; Vegetation; Anthropogenic activity; Asian countries; Built-up areas; Land-cover/land-use change; Management strategies; NDVI; Vegetation cover; Vegetation pattern; accuracy assessment; climate change; food production; food security; GIS; growing season; land cover; land surface; land use change; NDVI; remote sensing; surface temperature; Article; environmental management; environmental monitoring; environmental parameters; environmental temperature; geographic distribution; geographic information system; irrigation (agriculture); land surface temperature; land use; monsoon climate; normalized difference vegetation index; Pakistan; particle size; remote sensing; soil moisture; urbanization; vegetation; environmental monitoring; plant; procedures; season; temperature; Land surface temperature
AB  - Water and land both are limited resources. Current management strategies are facing multiple challenges to meet food security of an increasing population in numerous South Asian countries, including Pakistan. The study of land cover/land use changes (LCLUC) and land surface temperature (LST) is important as both provide critical information for policymaking of natural resources. We spatially examined LCLU and LST changes in district Multan, Pakistan, and its impacts on vegetation cover and water during 1988 to 2017. The LCLUC indicate that rice and sugarcane had less volatility of change in comparison with both cotton and wheat. Producer’s accuracy (PA) is the map accuracy (the producer of map), but user’s accuracy (UA) is the accuracy from the point of view of a map user, not the map maker. Average overall producer’s and user’s accuracy for the region was 85.7% and 87.7% for Rabi (winter) and Kharif (summer) seasons, respectively. The results of this study showed that ‘built-up area’ increased with 7.2% of all the classes during 1988 to 2017 in the Multan district. Anthropogenic activities decreased the vegetation, leading to an increase in LST in study area. Changes on LCLU and LST during the last 30 years have shown that vegetation pattern has changed and temperature has increased in the Multan district. © 2019, Springer Nature Switzerland AG.
