AU  - Sha, T.; Ma, X.; Zhang, H.; Janechek, N.; Wang, Y.; Wang, Y.; Castro García, L.; Jenerette, G.D.; Wang, J.
TI  - Impacts of Soil NOxEmission on O3Air Quality in Rural California
T2  - Environmental Science and Technology
LA  - English
KW  - Air Pollutants; Air Pollution; California; Humans; Iowa; Nitrogen Oxides; Ozone; Soil; United States; California; United States; Air quality; Budget control; Soils; nitrogen oxide; oxygen; sulfur oxide; nitrogen oxide; ozone; Anthropogenic sources; California; Cobenefits; Human health; NOx emissions; Rural regions; air quality; nitrous oxide; ozone; rural area; soil nitrogen; agricultural management; air quality; air temperature; Article; atmospheric deposition; California; circadian rhythm; concentration (parameter); cropland; desert; fertilizer application; forecasting; geographic mapping; irrigation (agriculture); land use; molecular size; nitrogen dynamics; nonhuman; oxygen concentration; precipitation; rural area; satellite imagery; soil chemistry; soil moisture; soil pollutant; soil temperature; summer; troposphere; weather; air pollutant; air pollution; human; Iowa; soil; United States; Nitrogen oxides
AB  - Nitrogen oxides (NOx) are a key precursor in O3 formation. Although stringent anthropogenic NOx emission controls have been implemented since the early 2000s in the United States, several rural regions of California still suffer from O3 pollution. Previous findings suggest that soils are a dominant source of NOx emissions in California; however, a statewide assessment of the impacts of soil NOx emission (SNOx) on air quality is still lacking. Here we quantified the contribution of SNOx to the NOx budget and the effects of SNOx on surface O3 in California during summer by using WRF-Chem with an updated SNOx scheme, the Berkeley Dalhousie Iowa Soil NO Parameterization (BDISNP). The model with BDISNP shows a better agreement with TROPOMI NO2 columns, giving confidence in the SNOx estimates. We estimate that 40.1% of the state's total NOx emissions in July 2018 are from soils, and SNOx could exceed anthropogenic sources over croplands, which accounts for 50.7% of NOx emissions. Such considerable amounts of SNOx enhance the monthly mean NO2 columns by 34.7% (53.3%) and surface NO2 concentrations by 176.5% (114.0%), leading to an additional 23.0% (23.2%) of surface O3 concentration in California (cropland). Our results highlight the cobenefits of limiting SNOx to help improve air quality and human health in rural California.  © 2021 The Authors. Published by American Chemical Society.
