AB  - This discourse commences with the acknowledgment of the widespread utilization of satellite data and the availability of various vegetation indices for vegetation identification. The Landsat-8 satellite imagery is specifically employed for the analysis discussed herein. The focus is on the rationality of choosing vegetation indices, such as NDWI (Normalized Difference Water Index) and NDMI (Normalized Difference Moisture Index), in contrast to the Wetness Index, for monitoring the growth of corn plants over time. Throughout different growth phases, from the initial planting to maturity, the distinctive patterns exhibited by NDWI and NDMI underscore their effectiveness in capturing crucial information related to plant and soil moisture content. The contextual preference for NDWI and NDMI over the Wetness Index is emphasized, showcasing their relevance in precision agriculture practices and their contribution to informed decision-making for optimal corn crop management.  © 2023 IEEE.
