AU  - Yang, Z.; Zhao, J.; Zhu, G.; Liu, J.; Guo, W.; Huang, Z.; Wang, Y.
TI  - Remote sensing estimation and spatial pattern analysis of soil moisture in Shiyang River Basin with vegetation coverage
T2  - Shengtai Xuebao
LA  - English
KW  - Geo-detector; Shiyang River Basin; Soil moisture trapezoidal characteristics; Spatial autocorrelation; China; Gansu; Shiyang Basin; agricultural land; bare soil; estimation method; grassland; irrigation system; remote sensing; soil moisture; soil-vegetation interaction; spatial distribution; vegetation cover; woodland
AB  - Soil moisture is the link between the surface and the atmosphere, and it plays a key role in vegetation growth and efficient agricultural irrigation. Taking the Shiyang River Basin as the research area, using the vegetation coverage/surface albedo trapezoidal characteristic spatial scatter plot to calculate the bare soil albedo, reducing the error of vegetation on the soil moisture obtained by remote sensing, and improving the accuracy of remote sensing soil moisture estimation. At the same time, the spatial pattern of SM and its influencing factors are analyzed through stability, spatial autocorrelation and geographic detectors. The results show that: (1) The SM inversion accuracy of the bare soil albedo model in the Shiyang River Basin is relatively high, which provides a new method for the calculation of SM at the basin scale. (2) SM has obvious spatial autocorrelation, Moran's value is 0.88 (Z- score=1852.94, P <0.01), the upstream woodland is high-high aggregation, the downstream desert is low-low aggregation, and SM is significantly correlated with FVC (P <0.01). (3) The overall stability of SM in the Shiyang River Basin during the year was good, and areas with good stability and good stability accounted for 88.34% of the study area. (4) The spatial distribution of SM is affected by multiple factors, and the explanatory power of each factor is significantly different. Among them, vegetation coverage> soil type> elevation> land use, and the interaction between factors enhances the explanatory power of the spatial differentiation of SM. (5) The SM of different land use types is quite different. Among them, most of the unused land is less than 7%; the SM of grassland and cultivated land is at the middle level, with an SM value of 7%-15%; the level of woodland is the highest, with an SM value greater than 25% . © 2020 Science Press. All rights reserved.
