AU  - Chandramohan, K.; Elayapillai, P.; Vijayalakshmi, G.; Kaliraj, S.
TI  - Evaluating the relation of NDVI, NDWI, SMI, and LAI to land and soil degradation processes — a case study of Virudhunagar district, Tamil Nadu, India
T2  - Remote Sensing of Soils: Mapping, Monitoring, and Measurement
LA  - English
KW  - LAI; land degradation; NDVI; NDWI; Remote sensing index; SAMI; South India; vulnerability analysis
AB  - Living organisms rely on land and soil for lithosphere–hydrosphere–atmosphere–biosphere interactions and to maintain environmental equilibrium. This study investigates the relationship between the normalized difference vegetation index (NDVI), normalized difference water index (NDWI), soil moisture index (SMI), and leaf area index (LAI) and land and soil degradation zones in the Virudhunagar district, Tamil Nadu, South India, at site-specific scale. The above parameters are calculated from the Landsat 8-OLI image using the different remote sensing indices. The GIS-based MEDALUS model is applied for mapping the degradation zones by analyzing multiple geoenvironmental parameters. The LDVI mapping for this area is illustrated in another chapter of this book by Kaliraj et al. (2023). Importantly, this analysis reveals the statistical relation of the vegetative indices mentioned above with land degradation zones at 30m×30m pixel level to provide a primary database for land and soil resource management. The result shows the NDVI is noted in the range of 0.577−0.114, wherein the higher value indicates the occurrence of healthy vegetation, mainly in upland forests and irrigated croplands in the western parts. Likewise, the NDWI is estimated at the range of 0.148–−0.517, and the areas having a lower value are denoted by higher vegetation–water stress, poor suitability of plant growth, and biomass rate, and it is especially found in the middle and eastern parts. Similarly, the SMI is calculated in the range from 0.373 to −0.422, thereby, the high rate is noted as severe soil–water stress for plant growth, while the higher value indicates that the land and soil contain enough soil moisture and water content that indicates good quality soils and support for plant growth. The LAI is estimated in the range of 1.362−0.314, which indicates the growth rate of plants or biomass, where the lowest values show poor vegetative cover. Significantly, the areas under critical LD class are related to lower NDVI, LAI, and NDWI, while the SMI is noted at a higher rate due to poor vegetation, biomass rate, soil moisture, and inadequate rainfall that drastically induce the processes of land and soil degradation in the long term. Importantly, the study area is gradually changing in LULC features, which affect land use productivity due to the activities of aridity, soil erosion, soil salinity, and other anthropogenic impacts. Major parts of the plains consist of unirrigated lands, fallows, barrens, and land with shrubs experiencing severe drought events happening in tropical regions like the study area. Analyzing the samples collected from typical sites indicates that the NDVI estimated as < 0.450, NDWI noted as −0.232, SMI value of < 0.012, and LAI value of < 0.618 were supporting and affecting the land degradation, as were all the indices coming under the nonaffected area (N). Based on our findings, future developments in machine learning should combine acceptability and sensibility. This will encourage researchers to expand their predictions of land degradation and analyze factors such as soil erosion, agroeconomic activity, and land use planning. © 2024 Elsevier Inc. All rights reserved.
